			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834583">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I'm sorry if I have no familiarity with the Bosnian war the Balkan MTF (is that an already existing MTF or is it a new one you made for this skip?) or what Balkan is.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hey <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/blazerboy69420" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5828787); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5828787&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606935914" alt="blazerboy69420" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5828787)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/blazerboy69420" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5828787); return false;">blazerboy69420</a></span>. As someone with a bit more familiarity with the Bosnian War, a lot of your critique here is fairly oblivious to how this story fits in to the themes and conflicts of the war. That time period in former Yugoslavia is famous for a heightened sense of nationalism and disapora, so things like ethnic Croats being born in Canada (thereby not having as direct a connection to Croatia itself), and a bar where ethnicity is not a characteristic one might be killed for are, quite frankly, very salient to the story that's trying to be told here.</p>
<p>Furthermore, a story structure similar to Life is Strange is not uncommon nor something that necessarily needs to be knocked here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Do you think the Chaos Insurgency just goes recruiting for agents on the fly? Sure I guess the mission stuff would cause a lot of animosity between Sam and Kosta but the fact that Sam somehow joined the CI because of it is literally so unrealistic, not just in-universe but irl. If you're working for the Foundation and you hear there's a splinter group that is actively engaging in attacks on the Foundation, who's firepower and military strength is larger than some countries, and they're actively terminating members of the CI as well, I wouldn't think twice of burying the possibility of me joining the CI to the ground.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The author is in sole control of the story they are telling here. If their conception of the CI is a strapped-for-manpower insurgent group that is desperate for members and is quick to recruit, then they are free to write that, so long as it makes sense in the setting and is sufficiently justified. You are also missing some pretty clear parallels here between the Foundation/CI and US allies/ insurgent groups during the Bosnian War.</p>
<p>If you're reading a pitch that is heavily mired in things you do not understand or have familiarity with, consider not critiquing them if you don't feel you are knowledgeable about the subject.</p>

							</div>

								
					
