			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834573">
									

<p>Okay so a lot to unpack here</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Would&nbsp;you&nbsp;like&nbsp;to&nbsp;know&nbsp;more?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Yes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The start of this is incredibly messy and hard to follow. Consider colour-coding your text, especially if you’re using updates, which are usually in blue.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I've read other articles which have crossed out info like this, so why exactly is it hard to read?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>See, this is confusing if we weren’t able to remember your labelling system. One option is to simply get rid of xxxx-5 label and mention the Karcist by name. OR convert his label to a PoI-designation, since he’s a separate humanoid entity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The labelling system seems pretty simple. There are 5 SCP entities, labelled SCP-XXXX-1 through 5. This does not seem very complicated to me. And, as far as I know, the Foundation never refers to anomalous individuals by their birth name other than maybe once in the description or if their properties require them to. Also, he is not a POI, he is an SCP. Simple as that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You’ve basically given away your entire narrative here, with testing logs, discovery logs and what I assume to be a final interview log with the Karcists all done and wrapped up.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Clearly, I didn't. This description tells you nothing about what happened to the town, where the people are and stuff like that. There are also no testing or discovery logs and there is no final interview with the Sarkics. So evidently I didn't give away my entire narrative as this assumption is entirely wrong.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Notice I still don’t know what these two incidents are referring to. They’re briefly mentioned earlier in the article but you didn’t specify what they were.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes&#8230;because they would give away the narrative? Something you told me not to do? So isn't that a good thing?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sorry, but what is this section supposed to be? Is it a log by the researcher? Uncovered documents? An interview with a Historian? I’m highly hesitant to read through a Wikipedia entry (basically an info-dump) as it’s likely to not contain any narrative or character development.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A brief summary of the history of the Cult and the town, says so at the start. And isn't it a good thing to give the reader context for how they got there?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is the first interesting part of the article, tbh. I always believe character should come first. Consider if you had simply moved this segment to the top of this section. Do you still need all that info when you can simply show what the town is like through the agent’s eyes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because if I did that then I would cut out all the information about the cult, its origins, how the US Gov found it and what kind of relationship they had.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It’s basically just producing monsters to fight in wars, yes? Eventually, this leads to conflict with the government (but not the Foundation?)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No. That isn't what happens, of all the cult members they send to war, only one makes creatures to actually attack. The rest are medics. Not to sound rude but statements like this make it sound like you either misread/misinterpreted my article or you did not read the whole thing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You’d need to majorly restructure this, however, so that it’s more in line with a traditional narrative.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As in a three-act structure? I do that. If you meant more like a normal book then that is impossible to do with this format.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If you’re playing with sarkicism, it’s essential to keep some of the mystery alive as we progress through the article. Make us wonder what’s going on instead of just telling us the facts from the start.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So you weren't even slightly curious about how a town of over 6000 people was reduced to just 5? Other than that, any suggestions for how to keep the mystery alive?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Have the Foundation investigate the claims and forget the US government, I feel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then the entire point of this story is lost. The whole point is that this SCP is supposed to show you WHY the government can't just handle this and WHY the UIU is so underfunded. If the Foundation simply looked into all of this then all that is lost. Furthermore, the Foundation would not do what the US gov did when dealing with the cult, so the story would need radical alterations which I do not want to make.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

